    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d12">

                <pb facs="0023" n="13" sameAs="frus:frus1941v05/pg13"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 P. W./68: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-01-12T11:00:00+09:00">January 12,
                            1941—11 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 12—7 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>51. Following is a summary of a telegram which my British colleague sent to the
                    Foreign Office at London on January 7: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>Germans in Tokyo discussing the immediate future of relations with Japan
                            emphasize the following points: The appointment of General <pb
                                facs="0024" n="14" xml:id="pg14"/> Oshima<anchor n="23"
                                xml:id="d12-f1"/> to Berlin is fully supported by the Japanese Navy
                            and is primarily strategic in objective; about the middle or later part
                            of February coincident with attempted German invasion of the British
                            Isles, Japan is to attack in the south, possibly Burma, “to knock out”
                            the British Empire; action is to be taken before United States is
                            prepared and so quickly that the United States will not be given time to
                            make decision for or against intervention—United States in any event
                            would face dilemma of deciding between military operations in Atlantic
                            and Pacific. Oshima may also carry Japanese proposals to Russia,
                            possibly concessions in China, since Tatekawa<anchor n="24"
                                xml:id="d12-f2"/> at Moscow has failed to accomplish anything.</p>
                        <p>According to the British Embassy’s informant who is fairly reliable, the
                            Japanese Navy is confident of initial success in a war with the United
                            States in the South Seas.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d12-f1" n="23" xml:id="d12-f1-content">
                <p>Gen. Hiroshi Oshima, formerly Japanese Military Attaché and then Ambassador in
                    Germany until 1939; reappointed December 20, 1940.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d12-f2" n="24" xml:id="d12-f2-content">
                <p>Gen. Yoshitsugu Tatekawa, Japanese Ambassador in the Soviet Union since September
                    1940.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
